| 2 | backward | [0,5] | [1,5] | 5 | 3 | [1, 3, 5, 2, 8, 7, 9] |
| 3 | forward | [1,5] | [1,4] | 4 | 2 | [1, 3, 2, 5, 7, 8, 9] |
| 4 | backward | [1,4] | [2,4] | 3 | 1 | [1, 2, 3, 5, 7, 8, 9] |
| 5 | forward; stop | [2,4] | [2,4] | 2 | 0 | [1, 2, 3, 5, 7, 8, 9] |

Sorted: [1, 2, 3, 5, 7, 8, 9]. Total comparisons 20; adjacent swaps 10.
\image(figures/cocktail_passes.svg)
// result_end
\caption(The first complete cycle fixes 9 on the right and 1 on the left. The table also retains the second cycle and the final two-comparison stopping scan.)
\alttext(Three native boxes show the original sequence, the actual first forward state and the actual first backward state. Arrows label six then five comparisons.)

Why is the result correct? Before a pass, the prefix before lo and suffix after
hi are already in their final sorted positions, with every middle value between
them. The running maximum or minimum justifies the next boundary update, so
the invariant persists. A zero-swap scan has inspected every adjacent pair in
the current window and found it ordered. Together with the invariant, that
makes the entire sequence ordered. An empty scan also certifies its singleton
window. Every successful pass shortens the window, so termination follows.

Swapping adjacent x>y removes their mutual inversion. Every third item is on
the same side of both before and after, so its total inversion contribution
does not change. Therefore, if I(a) is the initial strict inversion count,

\[
\text{total swaps}=I(a),\qquad
0\le I(a)\le\frac{n(n-1)}2.
\]
\alttext(The number of actual adjacent swaps equals the input's strict inversion count, between zero and n times n-minus-one divided by two.)

> The original window plot, including the stopping scan

Two complete cycles perform four directional passes and fix two positions at
each end. After the fourth pass the values are already sorted, but the kernel
has not yet certified that. It scans [2,4] forward, performs two comparisons
and stops. Thus five passes perform 6+5+4+3+2=20 comparisons and ten swaps.
The bounds need not meet. A directional pass and a full two-pass cycle are
different units; counting cycles alone hides the stopping work.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/cocktail_window.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  const auto t=dm::algorithms::cocktail_shaker_sort_trace({5,3,8,1,9,2,7});
  std::vector<double> pass{0},lower{0},upper{6};
  std::size_t completed_cycles=0;
  for(const auto& p:t.passes) {
    pass.push_back(pass.size());lower.push_back(p.next_lo);upper.push_back(p.next_hi);
    if(!p.forward)++completed_cycles;
  }
  std::print("| quantity | measured value |\n| :--- | ---: |\n| complete forward/backward cycles | {} |\n| directional passes including stop | {} |\n| final lo | {} |\n| final hi | {} |\n| comparisons in stopping scan | {} |\n",completed_cycles,t.passes.size(),t.passes.back().next_lo,t.passes.back().next_hi,t.passes.back().key_comparisons);
  auto plot=dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
  plot.line(pass,lower,"lo",{37,99,235},2).points(pass,lower,"lo",{37,99,235},6)
      .line(pass,upper,"hi",{220,38,38},2).points(pass,upper,"hi",{220,38,38},6)
      .legend({"lower bound","upper bound"},{{37,99,235},{220,38,38}})
      .title("Actual inclusive bounds: final scan leaves [2,4]")
      .x_label("completed directional passes; zero is input").y_label("array index")
      .x_limits(0,5.3).y_limits(-0.5,7).size(900,580)
      .save_svg("build/cocktail_window.svg");
}
```
