> Costs and limits of the API

The legacy output-only call takes its vector by value: an lvalue caller keeps
the original, while the kernel sorts its owned buffer with constant auxiliary
state. Calling it “in-place” describes the internal swaps, not a promise to
mutate the caller or avoid its input copy. Signed extreme integers are valid
because the kernel compares and exchanges values without subtracting them.

The optional trace owns every full value snapshot and original-index snapshot.
For p directional passes it uses O(np) storage and snapshot-copy work, in
addition to the sorting comparisons; p≤n, so tracing can use quadratic memory.
Counters do not measure those copies or elapsed time. Its default positive cap
of 1024 records is checked before each pass, including an empty terminal pass;
zero is invalid even for empty input. Exceeding the cap throws without returning
a partial trace. Callers can supply a different positive cap. The legacy
output-only call has no such cap. Empty/singleton inputs execute no passes.
Returned vectors own their contents and retain no references to the input.

> Conclusion

The original sequence needs two full cycles plus one stopping scan, with twenty
comparisons and ten swaps. Actual bounds stop before meeting. The mechanism
preserves sorted outer regions and equal-key identity order; every adjacent
swap removes exactly one inversion. Direction matters for comparison work:
the turtle family becomes linear, while reverse order remains quadratic and
the rabbit gains no comparison saving over a matched forward-only variant.
The evidence concerns exact key operations on controlled inputs, rather than
a claim about practical speed or every nearly sorted sequence.

> References

- P. E. Black and B. Bockholt, [Bidirectional bubble sort](https://xlinux.nist.gov/dads/HTML/bidirectionalBubbleSort.html), NIST Dictionary of Algorithms and Data Structures. The implementation here uses one-position bound updates and stops on the first zero-swap directional pass. The exact counts and identity arguments above are derived for the executing native kernel.
